Address_of does not escape an argument list.

MEASURED, and the two lines differ only in the ARGUMENT:

    struct CType* give(struct Sym* s) { return &s->type; }

    give(sp)->t       42          correct
    give(&sym)->t     <address>   the offset is added, the load is not

unary_expr sets Address_of when an expression begins with `&` and clears it
when it does not. An argument list is parsed in the middle of the postfix
chain, so an argument like `&sym` leaves the flag TRUE, and postfix_expr_arrow
then reads it:

    ... && (TRUE != Address_of || chain_continues)

and skips the load -- because a `&` at the end of a chain wants the address
rather than the value. The `&` belonged to the argument and was applied to the
member.

WHAT IT COST, AND WHY IT SURVIVED. tcc writes

    #define pointed_type(t)  (&(t)->ref->type)
    ...
    ((pointed_type(&p->type)->t & VT_BTYPE) == VT_VOID) &&
    0 == (pointed_type(&p->type)->t & (VT_CONSTANT | VT_VOLATILE))
                                                       tccgen.c:2807

-- an argument beginning with `&`, then `->t` on the result. That is the THIRD
clause of is_null_pointer, the one that recognises `(void*)0`, and it was
reading a pointer where VT_VOID belongs. So mc-tcc accepted

    (c ? 0 : s)->i          bare 0 -- caught by the FIRST clause

and rejected

    (c ? (void*)0 : s)->i   `error: struct or union expected`

which is tests2/33_ternary_op.c:37 and tests/tcctest.c:4082 -- the file test1,
test2 and test3 all begin with. It never faults: pointed_type is used
throughout tccgen for every pointer comparison and every type merge, and each
use quietly answers with an address instead of a type flag, far from where it
was asked.

HOW IT WAS FOUND. Not by reduction -- every hand-written model of the failing
construct passed, including the anonymous-union layout of Sym, pointed_type as
a real function, and the nested ternary inside the && chain. It took a probe
compiled INTO mc-tcc, printing that expression as 6897904 where 0 belongs, and
then printing 0 when the same expression was rewritten through a local.

SAME BOUNDARY AS THE COUNT ABOVE IT, and for the same reason. function_call
already saves and clears num_dereference_after_postfix across the argument
list, with a comment explaining that an argument list is a separate expression
context and is the only region that must not see the caller's pending state.
Address_of is the same hazard through the same door and gets the same
treatment. Saving the current value rather than restoring a constant keeps
`&f(x)->m` working, where the caller really did ask for an address.

MEASURED AFTER, locally:

    difftest amd64      97 pass, 0 fail     case 117 goes from KNOWN GAP to pass
    difftest aarch64    99 pass, 0 fail
    corpus  aarch64     419 of 426          unchanged
    33_ternary_op       compiles and RUNS, output matches tcc's .expect
    tcctest.c           COMPILES -- 4082 is gone. It now reaches the LINKER and
                        stops on __extendsftf2, strtof and __extenddftf2, which
                        are libtcc1's soft-float family and a libc symbol: the
                        two buckets the gen2 census already names, not a parse
                        or codegen gap.

--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -564,6 +564,47 @@ void function_call(struct token_list* s, int is_function_pointer)
 	int pending_deref = num_dereference_after_postfix;
 	num_dereference_after_postfix = 0;
 
+	/* AND Address_of IS THE SAME HAZARD, THROUGH THE SAME DOOR.
+	 *
+	 * unary_expr sets Address_of when it sees a leading `&` and clears it
+	 * when it does not -- so an ARGUMENT that begins with `&` leaves the
+	 * flag TRUE, and nothing between here and the postfix walk clears it.
+	 * postfix_expr_arrow then reads it:
+	 *
+	 *     ... && (TRUE != Address_of || chain_continues)
+	 *
